@@ -0,0 +1,72 @@
+# Release check blocked: `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` does not match the manifest
+
+## What I fixed
+
+`verify.py` had two bugs that made it fail on the real artifacts:
+
+1. It read each artifact in text mode and called `.encode()` on the result.
+   Binary artifacts are not valid UTF-8, so verification crashed with
+   `UnicodeDecodeError`. It now reads with `"rb"` and hashes the raw bytes.
+2. `SHA256SUMS` stores uppercase digests, but `hashlib` returns lowercase, so
+   even valid files were reported as mismatches. The expected digest is now
+   normalized to lowercase before comparison.
+
+With those fixes, `cli-2.0.1.zip` and `docs-2026.10.tar.gz` verify correctly,
+and `test_binary_files` and `test_reports_missing` pass.
+
+## Why the release check still cannot pass
+
+`SHA256SUMS` and `artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` genuinely disagree:
+
+```
+manifest:  7BEE020AC21EFB3831D920AF0B8C5EFE8D44CDF227AB94847F509188420356BD
+actual:    fde9b2575edbefb297fa7d93738b4bb1f09b7f777d1723b90194ad53cd9bc9ce
+```
+
+This is not a hashing/encoding artifact. I checked the obvious transformations
+(CRLF normalization, trailing newline, whitespace stripping, uppercase/lowercase
+content, git-blob framing) and none reproduce the manifest digest.
+
+The file is also not a usable archive:
+
+- `file` reports it as `data`, not gzip.
+- `tar tzvf artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` fails with
+  `Unrecognized archive format`.
+- Its bytes are unstructured data with no gzip header (`1f 8b`), unlike a
+  normal `.tar.gz`.
+
+There is no other copy of the artifact in the project or in git history
+(`git fsck` finds no dangling objects), so the original bytes needed to produce
+the manifest digest cannot be recovered.
+
+I did not "fix" this by rewriting `SHA256SUMS` to match the current bytes or by
+regenerating the tarball. Either would make the check pass while removing the
+integrity guarantee the release step exists to provide, and the current file is
+not a valid archive anyway.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+One of the following, from whoever owns the release artifacts:
+
+- re-upload/restore the correct `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` (the one whose SHA-256 is
+  `7BEE020A...356BD`), then re-run `python3 verify.py`; or
+- confirm the current artifact is authoritative and publish a new, reviewed
+  `SHA256SUMS` generated from it (ideally after replacing it with a valid
+  gzip/tar archive).
+
+Until then, `python3 verify.py` exits 1 and `test_release_artifacts_verify`
+fails with `['libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz']`. That failure is a real release blocker,
+not a bug in the verifier.
+
+## Current check status
+
+```
+$ python3 -m unittest -v test_verify.py
+test_binary_files ... ok
+test_reports_missing ... ok
+test_release_artifacts_verify ... FAIL ['libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz']
+```
+
+No change to `verify.py` can make these bytes hash to the manifest value: a
+SHA-256 digest is determined by the file contents. The two passing tests cover
+the verifier fixes; the third is blocked on the artifact above.
